  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186162083,
      "id": "96d48adf-a293-54ae-88fd-ee8471e5fc65",
      "title": "Applying Independence of Parametrization and Orientation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "bffda02e-242a-5e88-aa0a-9e5a29f9b6bc",
      ":question/problem": "Suppose ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=7$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=⟨{t}^{4}-2{t}^{2},\\,t⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le 3.$\n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-7$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=21,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(u)=⟨81{u}^{4}-18{u}^{2},\\,3u⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1.$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=7$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement I is false, while statement III is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=7.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=7.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\nx={y}^{4}-2{y}^{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "909270b2-87ad-5f53-bd5b-b130672fa733",
