    "question": {
      ":question/id": "547c6f64-88e9-5a47-8123-ac1c1a1e53ec",
      ":question/problem": "Suppose ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=6$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=⟨t,\\,4{t}^{3/2}⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le 9.$\n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=6$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-6$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=54,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(u)=⟨9u,\\,108{u}^{3/2}⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 1.$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=6.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=6.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we can convert each parametrization to Cartesian form. In doing so, we find that both parameterizations give the Cartesian curve\n\n$$\ny=4{x}^{3/2},\\,0\\le x\\le 9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
