    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "11dd4943-ad10-5a06-bb58-746889137f3b",
      ":question/problem": "Suppose ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=5$ for some function $f(x,y),$ where ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(u)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{u}{3}),\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{u}{3})⟩$ for $0\\le u\\le 3\\pi .$ \n\nWhich of the following must be true?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=30,$ where ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2} .$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=-5$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=5$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parametrization and orientation.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement. \n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are different parameterizations of the same curve. Since line integrals with respect to arc length are independent of the choice of parameterization, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=5.\n$$\n\n \nTo see that ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ represent the same curve, we note that both parameterizations traverse a half-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O$ between $0$ and $\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Reversing the curve's orientation does *not* change the value of a line integral with respect to arc length. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
